WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.390 --> 00:00:05.940
There are a lot of reasons why Stephen King's
It was ripe for revival on-screen in 2017.

00:00:05.940 --> 00:00:11.440
It'd been 27 years since the last adaptation,
which is a fateful number in the story.

00:00:11.440 --> 00:00:13.020
Clowns are still pretty damn scary.

00:00:13.020 --> 00:00:18.660
And, well, the 1990 TV miniseries simply couldn't
capture the most terrifying elements of King's

00:00:18.660 --> 00:00:22.039
bestseller under network restrictions and
a small-screen budget.

00:00:22.039 --> 00:00:26.570
The outdated special effects in particular
have rendered certain portions of the original

00:00:26.570 --> 00:00:29.210
It film laughable, instead of frightening.

00:00:29.210 --> 00:00:33.950
Even so, there are certain scenes in the 1990
miniseries that still hold up — and are

00:00:33.950 --> 00:00:37.860
actually even scarier than the new big-screen
adaptation.

00:00:37.860 --> 00:00:38.940
The moving pictures

00:00:38.940 --> 00:00:44.790
The 2017 version of It includes a memorably
terrifying sequence in which Pennywise comes

00:00:44.790 --> 00:00:50.230
to life within a sinister slideshow, but the
1990 version of that moment is a bit eerier

00:00:50.230 --> 00:00:51.600
in some respects.

00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:55.670
Bill Denbrough is alone and still completely
grief-stricken about his brother Georgie's

00:00:55.670 --> 00:00:58.660
funeral when he first comes into contact with
It.

00:00:58.660 --> 00:01:02.640
He skims through a book of photos, when all
of a sudden Georgie's most recent picture

00:01:02.640 --> 00:01:05.320
winks at him...and starts oozing blood.

00:01:05.320 --> 00:01:09.110
What's worse is that Bill's parents are more
concerned about him not belonging in the room

00:01:09.110 --> 00:01:12.970
than finding out what he saw — or more importantly,
what they can't see.

00:01:12.970 --> 00:01:16.610
It's nightmarish enough not to be believed
or heard by your parents, but when they're

00:01:16.610 --> 00:01:21.350
smearing invisible blood all over your murdered
brother's room it's even worse.

00:01:21.350 --> 00:01:25.579
Pennywise's page-bouncing also comes into
play later on when the Losers look at Mike's

00:01:25.579 --> 00:01:30.979
dad's photo album about Derry's many child-murder
sprees, and the town square's carnival comes

00:01:30.979 --> 00:01:34.010
to life with a certain dancing clown at the
center.

00:01:34.010 --> 00:01:38.719
If the slow burn of him coming into closer,
full-color view isn't scary enough, the fact

00:01:38.719 --> 00:01:43.079
that he then reaches through the page to grab
at the Losers should keep you up at night.

00:01:43.079 --> 00:01:44.299
Ben's father

00:01:44.299 --> 00:01:48.659
The new version of It makes very little of
Ben Hanscom's background, but the miniseries

00:01:48.659 --> 00:01:53.130
introduces a gnawing element of the story
that still works as a hair-raiser.

00:01:53.130 --> 00:01:57.579
Early on, Ben is seen traipsing through the
Barrens alone and sees his long-lost father

00:01:57.579 --> 00:02:02.070
in full military dress, standing in the swamp
and beckoning him into the sewer.

00:02:02.070 --> 00:02:06.710
The 2017 movie offers its own share of Pennywise's
dread-inducing impressions.

00:02:06.710 --> 00:02:11.290
But the idea that Ben's dead dad would slowly
morph into the clown like that is a kind of

00:02:11.290 --> 00:02:14.290
creepy cruelty that's hard to forget.

00:02:14.290 --> 00:02:15.290
Beverly's bathroom

00:02:15.290 --> 00:02:20.900
The 2017 movie does a good job of depicting
Beverly Marsh's infamous bloody bathroom — her

00:02:20.900 --> 00:02:25.090
physical maturation is stoking fear in Beverly
and others in her life.

00:02:25.090 --> 00:02:29.420
But there's still something extraordinarily
unsettling about the 1990 version.

00:02:29.420 --> 00:02:34.400
It's slower, there are children's voices crying
out to Beverly …

00:02:34.420 --> 00:02:35.080
"I'm Matthew O'Connor.

00:02:35.920 --> 00:02:38.020
We're all the dead kids."

00:02:38.260 --> 00:02:42.540
And once the balloon of blood explodes all
over the place, Mr. Marsh puts his hands right

00:02:42.550 --> 00:02:46.379
into the gory mess, unable or unwilling to
see what his kid sees.

00:02:46.379 --> 00:02:50.099
So, it's a test of her squeamishness and sanity.

00:02:50.099 --> 00:02:51.530
Eddie's shower

00:02:51.530 --> 00:02:56.670
There's no denying that Eddie Kaspbrak's first
encounter with It in the 2017 version is really

00:02:56.670 --> 00:02:57.670
creepy.

00:02:57.670 --> 00:03:01.500
But there's a certain emotional vulnerability
that's lost in translation from the first.

00:03:01.500 --> 00:03:06.319
In the first version, Eddie is being pinballed
by the adults in his life — his mother has

00:03:06.319 --> 00:03:11.129
told him not to shower at school, but his
gym teacher absolutely insists he does.

00:03:11.129 --> 00:03:14.980
Everyone else has already cleaned up by the
time Coach wins the argument, which leaves

00:03:14.980 --> 00:03:19.299
Eddie out to dry solo in the school showers
— just as they start to come to life.

00:03:19.299 --> 00:03:23.950
Mid-rinse, all the nozzles start operating
themselves and turn every spout into a scalding

00:03:23.950 --> 00:03:29.070
torture device meant to steer Eddie away from
safety just before Pennywise pays him a visit

00:03:29.070 --> 00:03:30.070
through the drain.

00:03:30.070 --> 00:03:34.629
If Psycho didn't make you think twice about
showering away from home, here's another scene

00:03:34.629 --> 00:03:37.629
that'll creep into your memory at the worst
moment.

00:03:37.629 --> 00:03:38.769
Going clear

00:03:38.769 --> 00:03:42.739
The kids' final stand against Pennywise in
the new film is incredibly similar to what

00:03:42.739 --> 00:03:44.480
goes down in the miniseries.

00:03:44.480 --> 00:03:49.330
And both ignore the novel's very weird Ritual
of Chüd in favor of a more old-fashioned

00:03:49.330 --> 00:03:53.739
fight with It, and make their pact to come
back together if and when It returns.

00:03:53.739 --> 00:03:57.819
But despite all the faults to be found in
the camerawork, props, and visual effects,

00:03:57.819 --> 00:04:02.700
the 1990 version is still highly effective,
thanks to some key fog machine work and the

00:04:02.700 --> 00:04:04.170
attack on poor Stan.

00:04:04.170 --> 00:04:08.629
While the Losers are trying to stay together,
Pennywise already sees the weakest links in

00:04:08.629 --> 00:04:11.360
their chain and tries to make a meal out of
Stanley Uris.

00:04:11.360 --> 00:04:15.819
It's a terrible preview of what's to come,
and the fact that Pennywise is able to break

00:04:15.819 --> 00:04:19.390
their chain certainly undermines their sense
of strength in numbers.

00:04:19.390 --> 00:04:22.450
"I am eternal child."

00:04:22.450 --> 00:04:23.910
The meta element

00:04:23.910 --> 00:04:28.470
One of the essential elements of the first
movie is how informative pop-culture was to

00:04:28.470 --> 00:04:29.670
the Losers' fear.

00:04:29.670 --> 00:04:33.700
For example, within days of the group seeing
I Was a Teenage Werewolf at the Paramount

00:04:33.700 --> 00:04:38.910
theater, the group's most entertainment-oriented
member, Richie Tozier, is chased through the

00:04:38.910 --> 00:04:41.940
school's basement by a werewolf in a varsity
jacket.

00:04:41.940 --> 00:04:46.730
That scene speaks volumes about Stephen King's
story and influences, as it derives from so

00:04:46.740 --> 00:04:50.520
many other scares while also informing new
nightmares all its own.

00:04:52.140 --> 00:04:55.180
"They float, Georgie.

00:04:56.560 --> 00:04:57.800
They float."

00:04:57.800 --> 00:04:59.400
Thanks for watching!

